==English==
{{was wotd|2008|August|17}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|cisterne|lang=fro}} (Modern French {{term|citerne|lang=en}}) from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|cisterna|lang=la}}, from {{term|cista||box|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|κίστη||box|tr=kistē|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈsɪs.tən/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈsɪs.tɚn/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-cistern.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪstə(ɹ)n|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[reservoir]] or [[tank]] for holding [[water]], especially for [[catching]] and [[holding]] [[rainwater]] for later use.
#* '''1913''', A.C. Cotter, ''[[w:Catholic Encyclopedia|Catholic Encyclopedia]]'', "[[s:Catholic Encyclopedia (1913)/Wells in Scripture|Wells in Scripture]]",
#*: Their extreme necessity is attested by the countless number of old, unused '''cisterns''' with which the Holy Land is literally honeycombed.
#* {{quote-book
|year=2001
|author=Philip J. King and Lawrence E. Stager
|title=Life in Biblical Israel
|chapter=3
|isbn=0664221483
|page=126
|passage='''Cisterns''' (''bôr'', ''bō'r''), mentioned frequently in the Bible, are artificial reservoirs, usually cut into bedrock, for collecting and conserving rain runoff from roofs and courtyards.}}
# {{context|technical|lang=en}} In a [[flush toilet]], the container in which the water used for [[flush]]ing is held; a toilet tank.
#* {{quote-book
|year=2003
|author=Allan Windust
|title=Waterwise House & Garden: a Guide for Sustainable Living
|chapter=9
|isbn=0643068007
|page=36
|passage=It is possible to connect your tank to your toilet '''cistern''' and/or garden, so that even if the water is not drinkable it still can be used productively to make major water savings.}}
# {{context|anatomy|lang=en}} A [[cisterna]].

